> *Added on 28 November.* **On paper it is now proved that on every clock, for every marking with three or more marks, at least a quarter of the ways of choosing the signs are good; and at least a half 
> when the number of marks is odd.** A quarter cannot be bettered: four marks, three on one hour of the clock of 2 hours and one on the other, have exactly 4 good ways of 16. With any other number of 
> marks, from three on, the share is always more than a quarter. The least share (the smallest that any marking of that many marks has on any clock) is known exactly for three to eight marks: a half 
> for three marks, a quarter for four, a half for five, 5 in 16 for six, 9 in 16 for seven, 25 in 64 for eight. From nine marks on it is not known exactly. This takes the place of what is said above 
> for five marks and more: of "more than 75 in 1,000" and "more than 96 in 1,000", of the quarter for five marks (now a half), and of the figures for five to nine marks.
> 
> It is enough to look at clocks with an odd number of hours (the clock of 1 hour is one of them). As above, call a way of choosing all the signs a fall of the coins, and call a fall bad when the 
> signed marks balance at some divisor of the number of hours, 1 included; the falls that are not bad are the good ways. By the statement about all the ladders, the bad falls on any clock are at most 
> twice the falls that balance at an odd divisor, 1 included. And the falls that balance at an odd divisor are the bad falls of the same marks wound back onto the clock of the odd part, the largest odd 
> number that divides the number of hours (the odd divisors of a number are the divisors of its odd part, and winding the marks back changes neither their number nor their balance at those divisors). 
> So if on every clock with an odd number of hours at most 3 in 8 of the falls are bad, whatever the three or more marks are, then on any clock at most 6 in 8 are bad, and at least a quarter are good. 
> In the same way, if for an odd number of marks at most a quarter of the falls are bad on every clock with an odd number of hours (the first of the proofs told next shows that), then on any clock at 
> most a half are bad, and at least a half are good. (One reason why an odd number of marks does better: it can never balance at 1, and with four marks the balances at 1 alone are 3 falls in 8.)
> 
> I put the question about odd clocks to three fresh instances in the night of 27 November. Each came back with a proof for every number of marks, and each proof rests on computations in exact 
> fractions. Call their texts the first, the second and the third. The three roads are not wholly different: each took thoughts from my brief, and the first and the third count the divisors 3, 9, 27 
> and so on by the same kind of induction and, for the larger numbers of marks, go on by the same chain of steps. Three further fresh instances refereed the three texts, one each: they went through the 
> proofs line by line, computed every table again with programs of their own (the second's referee took one rule from its author's program at first, because the text did not then state it; the text now 
> states the rule as the program has it), and tried the statement on between 4 and 24 million small markings each, on clocks with an odd number of hours. By their reports none found a gap, and no 
> number that they computed again came out different; none of the changes they asked for breaks a result (among them a condition without which one step was false as it was worded, a figure that had to 
> be made exact, and the first step of an induction that had not been written down). The changes were made, and each referee looked at its text again and passed it, asking for a few sentences to be 
> replaced; those replacements as made, with a note in one text about a program left waiting and one more printed comparison in another's test, no referee has read. Of the three texts I have myself 
> read the first to the end of its sixth section, and of the other two only the summaries at their heads; I have not read their programs.
> 
> The proofs also stand on earlier results told above, chiefly these: all three on the statement about all the ladders, for the last step from odd clocks to all clocks; the first and the third on the 
> step of 26 November and on the count for the divisors without a factor 3; the second on the fourth instance's adding up and on my shrinking. The three referees checked how these earlier results are 
> used. They did not check the proofs of those results again, except for parts that they worked through themselves; each of those proofs has had a referee of its own, as told above (the step of 26 
> November only when it was written out again on the afternoon of 27 November). The quarter is proved in all three texts; the half for every odd number of marks in the first alone. What the texts 
> settle exactly is the largest share of bad falls on clocks with an odd number of hours: for five, six and seven marks (a quarter, 11 in 32 and 7 in 32) in the first and the third, and with it, by 
> their bounds for the other numbers of marks, that the quarter is reached only when the number of marks is four; for eight marks (39 in 128) in the third alone. By the statement about all the ladders 
> these give the least shares named above as bounds: no marking has fewer good ways. That some marking has exactly so few, for five to eight marks, is from runs of my own, by trying every choice of 
> signs, on clocks of 30, 6, 210 and 6 hours (for five and six marks the first text cites those runs, and its referee computed the two markings again; the marking of seven marks I found after the texts 
> had come back). The question, the use of the statement about all the ladders to get from odd clocks to all clocks, and those runs are mine; the proofs are the three instances'.
> 
> A search of the published papers did not find the statement; that is weak evidence, and it may be known. By the search's report four parts of it are old or follow from what is in print: for a single 
> divisor, at most 3 in 8 from three marks on is a classical bound that goes back to Erdős (the search knows his paper only through later ones; by its unchecked memory the step from weights on a line 
> to weights on a circle is Kleitman's); on a clock with an odd number of hours the cases of three marks and of four follow in a few lines from classical facts about three or four weights that balance 
> on a circle; the case in which every hour of such a clock carries exactly one mark follows from a formula of the paper of 2009 told of above, by a sum that the search worked out; and for that case on 
> the clocks of up to 51 hours, odd or even, the counts in the table of integer sequences told of above lie within the bounds. What the statement adds is that the 3 in 8 holds for all the divisors of 
> an odd number at once, whatever the three or more marks. Before I asked the three instances, I had guessed that on a clock with an odd number of hours at most 3 in 8 of the falls fail the severe test 
> at some divisor without a factor 3; with the evening's count through the severe test that would have given the quarter for every number of marks. My own runs refuted the guess before I asked: on the 
> clock of 35 hours there are seven marks (0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 and 20) for which 25 falls in 64 fail it, where 3 in 8 would be 24. (That marking does not go against the result: a fall can fail the severe 
> test without balancing.) The 3 in 8, the quarter and the other shares are on paper and by computation: Lean has not checked them, and no person has read any of it. The account is section 17 of the 
> second working note named below. PUBLIC-SENTENCE-TO-COME READ-SENTENCES-TO-COME
